NETRUN per-GB 3proxy: 3proxy 0.9.3 (upstream tag 0.9.3) + nine changes.
Applied with `patch -p1` in the extracted 3proxy-0.9.3/ tree by
scripts/build_3proxy_pergb.sh; the result is deploy/node/bin/3proxy-pergb
(sha256 pinned in deploy/node/bin/3proxy-pergb.sha256). Per-piece batches keep
the stock deploy/node/bin/3proxy.

1. conf.c h_authcache: `authcache none` sets authcachetype 0. doauth()
   (auth.c) inserts a cache entry after every successful auth whenever the type
   is non-zero and never prunes it (only cacheauth() prunes, and it runs only
   with `auth cache`): one leaked entry per distinct login with the default
   type 6, one per connection with `acl`, each insert a walk of the whole list
   under the global hash mutex. Upstream turns 0 into 6, so the cache could not
   be switched off from the cfg.
2. common.c (connectwithpoll): after poll() reports the non-blocking connect
   finished, check SO_ERROR before declaring success. poll() returns the socket
   writable on a FAILED connect too (POLLOUT|POLLERR for an RST or an
   unreachable host), so stock 3proxy answers "200 Connection established" (and
   a SOCKS success) for a dead upstream, then the relay closes at once — a
   client sees a TLS error instead of an honest 502/SOCKS reject, and proxy
   checkers read the dead tunnel as alive. A non-zero SO_ERROR now fails the
   connect (code 13 -> 502 / SOCKS reply 5).
3. common.c (outbound connect): IP_BIND_ADDRESS_NO_PORT before the bind of the
   outbound socket, so the source port is chosen at connect() for the full
   4-tuple: sockets from one egress address to different destinations share
   source ports instead of each holding one of ip_local_port_range.
4. common.c (doconnect, #4 OS fingerprint): from the login's -os-<profile>, set
   the outbound hop limit (128 for windows, 64 for macos — IP_TTL /
   IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS, no privilege) and SO_MARK (1 windows, 2 macos) for the
   egress fingerprint rewriter (deploy/node/osfp); SO_MARK needs CAP_NET_ADMIN
   and fails harmlessly without it (only the TTL applies). The window / scale /
   option / timestamp rewrite is done by that rewriter, not in 3proxy.
5. socks.c (the SOCKS reply code): upstream maps a result >= 100 to
   res % 10, so res 100 — no destination, the name did not resolve (every
   IPv4-only site once the node is -6, IPv6 only since 2026-10-14) — became
   reply 0 = success: the client read "request granted" and got an empty
   tunnel. 100 now answers 4 (host unreachable), any other res % 10 == 0
   answers 1 (general failure). SOCKS4 follows (91 rejected).
6. socks.c (SOCKS listener): consume an optional HAProxy PROXY v2 header at the
   start of sockschild (the per-GB haproxy sends send-proxy-v2 on the
   pergb_socks backend; loopback-only listener, trusted) and keep the real
   client and the public address it dialed for the UDP relay ONLY: sincr /
   sincl stay the loopback hop, which the log (%C %c %i), RADIUS and the node
   agent's meter and kills match on. A SOCKS greeting (first byte 5/4) never
   matches the magic (0x0D), so a connection without the header is untouched.
7. socks.c (SOCKS5 UDP ASSOCIATE relay, owner 2026-10-14: UDP on the same
   ports): the relay's client socket binds to that public address, advertises
   it and accepts datagrams only from the real client; its egress socket takes
   the family of the egress RADIUS assigned, not of the client's 0.0.0.0:0
   placeholder (an IPv4 socket on a dual-stack listener, which the piece's
   IPv6 could not bind). A datagram to a name
   without an address of the node's family (an IPv4-only host on an IPv6-only
   node), of an unknown address type or of a family the egress socket can not
   send to, to a special address (loopback, private, link-local, multicast,
   v4-mapped) or to a scanner / reflection port (0 19 137-139 1900 11211), or
   over 20000 per second per association, is dropped and the association
   lives on (upstream ended it on a bad one, sent the rest blindly). Interim log
   records at the logdump thresholds (either direction), as a TCP relay
   writes them, so a long association is billed while it lasts. A refused
   ASSOCIATE (RADIUS, ACL) gets its SOCKS reply (upstream wrote it to a relay
   control socket that did not exist yet, so the client saw only a close). The login is asked again every 300 s,
   so an expired or switched-off proxy loses its association (the node agent
   kills TCP sockets only). Which logins may open one is RADIUS's call
   (per-piece proxies only, for now).
8. proxy.c / socks.c (no 3proxy fingerprint for whoever connects to the
   ports): the HTTP proxy's answers are our own short plain-text pages
   (HTTP/1.1, Content-Length, realm "Netrun", the cause in X-Proxy-Error)
   instead of 3proxy's HTML ("Access to requested resource disallowed by
   administrator..."), and a refused SOCKS request carries 0.0.0.0:0 instead
   of the listener's own 127.0.0.4:<port>.
9. authradius.c (radsend): upstream binds param->remsock — the client's
   outbound socket — to the RADIUS server's configured local address; a SOCKS5
   UDP ASSOCIATE creates that socket before it asks RADIUS, and on an IPv4
   socket bind(AF_UNSPEC 0.0.0.0) succeeds, so the relay's own bind to its
   egress then failed (code 12). The RADIUS socket is bound instead, and only
   when a local address is configured.

